#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a898f345-9210-5976-9a42-f415a0e7eaf5" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables in a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "d123b7fd-5a13-50c0-aa8c-dc196f4b1bcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12406" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in Cartesian $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:1 \\leq xy \\leq  2, \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z\\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\nwrite down the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_R 3xy^2+6yz \\, \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c03fec53fda9106ca287f15afd0d07b1adddf00675b2bdbec8a658fbdf06aa.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/64/643fb7a9bbc46ac2a6fe8c6d89f4fcb7f2db206c7af11f7cde9e1a34da8ac201.png)\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(y-0) \\\\  & =2y \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3x{y}^{2}+6yz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(3uv+3vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3v(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "832dc2b8-873f-56c6-bd87-5e886a586fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Using a Change of Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "3fccb151-d67f-525b-8804-62589913084a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12407" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint \\limits_R (x+y^2)^{15}z \\: \\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:  0 \\leq x+y^2 \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y+z \\leq 4, \\: 0\\leq z\\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/eb/eb8f40c6ffabf36ae5a305c7c7b71683b914b537f64766a20aac09d3666fac81.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/4b/4bcd5430181c2cb411dfe984b04399f2f80ab9d1747d5cf82596d105419b2b4f.png)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{15}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}\\cdot \\frac{w}{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{15}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{15}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{16}}{16}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=4}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a79d4e-f9e0-583e-8e52-aa60c14fd4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Region Using a Change of Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "e00a8e15-e738-5fee-80f7-405844fa1422" :question/math-academy-id "e-12408" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\:-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8 \\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/54/54035a0d0a2dd88eb0b4b89bb1162df2cf9b450a4ea4d5b5020163053880016f.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\qquad\n-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\qquad\n1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y, \\qquad\nv = y-2x, \\qquad\nw = z^3.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/4b/4bcd5430181c2cb411dfe984b04399f2f80ab9d1747d5cf82596d105419b2b4f.png)\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac 19 w^{-2/3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-2}^{2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-(-2))\\cdot 3({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "832dc2b8-873f-56c6-bd87-5e886a586fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Using a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f646c61-db32-5905-849a-04ada136657a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194370} {:answer/id #uuid "dda01489-76a5-59ec-9704-87adeb52fd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194371} {:answer/id #uuid "18c290d7-6b8d-50ae-99c2-f0eb7045b012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194372} {:answer/id #uuid "1935b921-c755-5f9b-b2fd-fdf3102bd3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194373} {:answer/id #uuid "494337ce-d303-53d0-97cd-b0e1b611d95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194374}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5cc458-53cc-5d59-888b-d4167a5b9c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194369}] :question/id #uuid "1d5d9726-730c-531d-b668-66f3dda1040e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162139" :question/problem "Use the change of variables\n$u = 3x - 2y, v = y + 2z, w = z$\nto evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)(3x - 2y)^{2}(y + 2z)dV$,\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le 3x - 2y \\le 3, 0 \\le y + 2z \\le 2, 0 \\le 2z \\le 1}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a898f345-9210-5976-9a42-f415a0e7eaf5" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efccd2f5-3dcb-549b-a791-9a953c0859ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197649} {:answer/id #uuid "01110ca9-4f16-5a77-ade5-867fbcd28671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197650} {:answer/id #uuid "28f56d8e-e1c7-5447-a918-701e450eabeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197651} {:answer/id #uuid "46f32d0b-96ea-5d4d-8468-c29158bdc3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197652} {:answer/id #uuid "721547b0-a678-5c59-b301-7880bb72ccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197653}] :answer-field/id #uuid "92bb2c37-88bf-542c-b72c-3da1ea32bcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197648}] :question/id #uuid "9d27a7ac-3737-5071-b997-af83c998bd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39054" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): \\frac{3π}{4} \\le 2x + y \\le π, 0 \\le y + z \\le \\frac{π}{2},-1 \\le z \\le 1}$.\nGiven the change of variables\n$u = 2x + y, v = y + z, w = z$,\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n$∭_(R)\\sin (2x + y)\\cos (y + z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{3π/4}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}A A A A A Adwdvdu$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "832dc2b8-873f-56c6-bd87-5e886a586fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Using a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef7332e4-b981-54af-8da2-fcdb494e9902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197655} {:answer/id #uuid "281c6df6-036f-5860-be8c-11e9d432022a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197656} {:answer/id #uuid "467be8d9-d070-5455-96ad-bb6a53f6902c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln 2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197657} {:answer/id #uuid "7557b355-b5e6-5c35-9ec5-ce7baaeba82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197658} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c87e9-0a3c-552b-81b8-0ac086467daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197659}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb66677-2e9a-5652-8302-63d8739cb7ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197654}] :question/id #uuid "3982f9b1-ca56-5e4b-8f97-4f23fb6f2fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39055" :question/problem "Use the change of variables\n$u = x^{2} + y, v = x + z, w = z$\nto evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)(x + z)\\cos (x^{2} + y)dV$,\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): \\frac{π}{6} \\le x^{2} + y \\le \\frac{π}{2}, 0 \\le x + z \\le 2,-1 \\le z \\le 1}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a79d4e-f9e0-583e-8e52-aa60c14fd4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Region Using a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881b0dd0-01c6-5013-9685-0e58c6da71c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197661} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a11a8a-6503-5229-93d1-630340faec00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197662} {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} {:answer/id #uuid "3404d280-8cad-5644-8ec1-3e6e08824288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197664} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd7243b-48c8-50fa-aaa2-6aa1ef439e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197665}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd3b3e1-98ae-532b-94b3-c5b9b8ab641b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197660}] :question/id #uuid "8b45928b-ff82-5faf-a3d8-f16998390abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39060" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 1 \\le y \\le 2, 1 \\le \\sqrt{y} + x \\le 2, 1 \\le \\frac{1}{z + 1} \\le 5}$."}]}
